  "Butterwort"
 ],
 "description": "This is a genus of ca. 130 species of carnivorous herbaceous perennials in the bladderwort family (Lentibulariaceae). Butterwort can be found growing in sunny, open, wet locations throughout the southeastern US. They do well in nutrient-poor soil. The genus name is derived the from the Latin word \" pinguis ,\" meaning fat or greasy, along with the suffix \"- ula ,\" meaning little. \"Little greasy one\" references the greasy feel to the leaves. Grow in full sun to partial shade in consistently moist soil. In containers, use a planting mix of whole-fiber sphagnum moss or a mix of equal parts peat moss or coir and coarse vermiculite or sharp sand. A dormancy period is necessary to regrow and bloom each spring. Cut back dead leaves in the winter or early spring to encourage new growth. Small insects are trapped and digested on the greasy surface of the leaves. The insects serve as nutrients for the plant in habitats where the soil is very poor in nutrients. The trap is a sticky-trap, like flypaper; the leaves do not move. Grow butterwort in a carnivorous plant collection, a native garden, pollinator garden or any sunny, ever-wet area. Insects, Diseases, and Other Plant Problems: none.",
